# Git hooks are run by `sh`. A CRLF here makes the shebang line read
# `#!/bin/sh\r`, which fails with a bare "no such file or directory" that
# names nothing — keep them LF on every platform.
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# THE MIRRORED SET IS PINNED TO LF (R08, 2026-08-17).
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true here, so the working tree is CRLF while git stores
# LF. The Drive mirror is written by copying the WORKING TREE, so a mirror copy
# produced by anything that writes LF — a non-Windows checkout, a different
# tool, an editor that normalizes on save — could never again compare equal to
# the CRLF copy beside it. The comparison in scripts/handoff.mjs now normalizes
# line endings, which fixes the symptom; this fixes the cause, so the two sides
# agree by construction rather than by both remembering to normalize.
#
# The measured case: CLAUDE.md is 45417 bytes in the working tree and 45147 in
# git, and the 270-byte gap is exactly its 270 CRLF line endings.
#
# This list is the text half of MIRRORED_DOCS in scripts/mirror-set.mjs. That
# module stays authoritative for WHAT is mirrored; this only fixes HOW those
# files are stored on disk. A file added there should be added here too.
CLAUDE.md            text eol=lf
docs/abuse-policy.md text eol=lf
docs/vendor-review.md text eol=lf
docs/source-ladder.md text eol=lf
docs/defects.md      text eol=lf
docs/state.md        text eol=lf
docs/tracker.csv     text eol=lf
docs/deferred.md     text eol=lf
docs/deferred.csv    text eol=lf
lib/sources/types.ts text eol=lf

# The workbook is the one binary in the mirrored set. Marking it explicitly
# keeps any future `text` wildcard from ever applying newline translation to
# it, which would corrupt the file rather than merely mis-compare it.
docs/deferred.xlsx   binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE WHOLE TREE IS LF ON EVERY PLATFORM (Prompt C item 1b, 2026-08-20).
#
# Before this line the repo had no default, so what a text file looked like on
# disk depended on which tool last wrote it. `core.autocrlf` is true here, so
# git STORES LF and checks out CRLF — but anything a script wrote after
# checkout stayed LF. Measured on 2026-08-20: of 970 tracked text files, 336
# carried CRLF in the working tree and 634 carried LF. `data/reference/` was
# the case that surfaced it — all seven data CSVs CRLF, all seven provenance
# notes LF, and a reconciliation between two copies of the repo that differed
# by nothing but line endings.
#
# Every consumer of that difference has to normalize or lie: the mirror
# comparison in scripts/handoff.mjs, the reference reconciler, every byte-size
# figure in a report, every hash. Fixing them one at a time is fixing symptoms.
# This fixes the cause — the working tree matches what git stores, so Windows
# and Linux hold identical bytes and there is nothing left to normalize.
#
# Content in git does not move: it was already LF. This changes only the
# CHECKOUT, which is why adding it produced no staged diff.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The binaries, named rather than left to git's NUL sniffing. Auto-detection
# is good and it is not a guarantee, and a text conversion applied to one of
# these corrupts the file rather than merely mis-comparing it.
*.jpg  binary
*.png  binary
*.ico  binary
*.xlsx binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# RECORDED EVIDENCE IS NEVER CONVERTED (Prompt C item 1b, 2026-08-20).
#
# A recorded fixture is the bytes a server actually sent, and the whole point
# of committing it is that a parse test runs against the real thing. Applying
# the tree-wide LF rule above to these would rewrite that evidence: it caught
# `tests/fixtures/civicplus-rockaway-agendacenter.html`, stored with 2,555 CRLF
# line endings because CivicPlus sent them, on the very first renormalization.
# `-text` means no conversion in either direction — checkout and commit both
# hand over the stored bytes unchanged.
#
# `data/reference/` is the same argument: those seven CSVs and their
# provenance notes are the published federal files, and their reconciliation
# is a byte comparison.
tests/fixtures/**  -text
data/reference/**  -text

# Preserve the original bytes of provisional reader-proof receipts, including
# their historical hashes. Current large receipts are already lossless gzip.
docs/evidence/provisional-bde7497/*.json -text whitespace=cr-at-eol
docs/evidence/provisional-e504/*.json -text whitespace=cr-at-eol
